<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">FET y de las JONS</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Falange Española Tradicionalista y de las JONS</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">FET y de las JONS was the single party of Francoist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>'s decree of unification in April 1937, which merged the fascist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falange-espanola-de-las-jons">Falange Española de las JONS</a> with the Carlist traditionalists under his own leadership. It controlled the official trade unions, youth and women's organisations and propaganda, though its influence declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Later known as the National Movement, it was dissolved in 1977.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fet-y-de-las-jons.jpg" alt="FET y de las JONS" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Az88 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoke_and_Arrows.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party had its roots in formations of the Spanish right in the early 1930s. The Falange Española was founded in October 1933 by José Antonio Primo de Rivera, son of the former military dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera</a>, as a self-consciously fascist movement. The Juntas de Ofensiva Nacional-Sindicalista were a national syndicalist organisation led by Ramiro Ledesma Ramos and Onésimo Redondo. The two merged in February 1934 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falange-espanola-de-las-jons">Falange Española de las JONS</a>, which probably had no more than ten thousand members before the Civil War. With the war from July 1936 it grew within months to several hundred thousand, and veterans derisively called the new recruits camisas nuevas, new shirts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In April 1937 a power struggle between Hedilla and rivals threatened the Nationalist coalition. Franco used the crisis to issue the Unification Decree on 19 April 1937, which forcibly merged the Falange with the Traditionalist Communion; all other parties in the Nationalist zone were dissolved by omission. Franco took the title of jefe nacional. The party kept 26 of the 27 points of the Falangist platform, with the blue shirt, the yoke and arrows and the anthem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol</a>.</p><p>After the Nationalist victory in 1939 the party served as the regime's institutional vehicle and as a career structure, and membership peaked at around 932,000 in 1942. After the Allied victory in 1945 the post of Secretary General was abolished, in part to signal distance from the regime's fascist past, and the party was reactivated from 1948. From the late 1950s its role became ceremonial, as technocrats linked to Opus Dei set economic policy. After Franco's death in November 1975 the party and its institutions were dissolved on 7 April 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Membership was automatic for civil servants and military officers, though often nominal. Scholars describe Francoism as a coalition of competing families: Falangists, Carlists, the military, National Catholics and technocrats. Historians disagree about its ideology: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-linz">Juan José Linz</a> argued that Francoism was not fully fascist.</p><p>The Sección Femenina, the Frente de Juventudes and the Spanish Syndical Organization were the main way the regime reached most people. The Syndical Organization, the sindicato vertical, was the compulsory labour structure that replaced the independent trade unions dissolved in 1938, grouping workers and employers by industry rather than by class.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “FET y de las JONS” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the Falange Española de las JONS, whose founder José Antonio Primo de Rivera had been executed by the Republican government in November 1936, and merged it with the Carlist Comunión Tradicionalista into the <b>Falange Española Tradicionalista y de las JONS</b>, the main component of the Movimiento Nacional.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Francoist Spain the Falange was merged with other far-right groups into the <b>Falange Española Tradicionalista y de las JONS</b>, the only legal party, and &quot;Cara al Sol&quot; became a &quot;canto nacional&quot; (national song) together with the Carlist &quot;Oriamendi&quot; and the anthem of the Spanish Legion, often played…</blockquote><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol</a></li><li><blockquote>Franco ruled through the army, the Church and a single party, <b>FET y de las JONS</b>, with harsh repression, executions and labour camps in the 1940s.</blockquote><a href="/state/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain</a></li></ul></section>
